at", -3.254361391067505]]}, {"t": " kernel", "lp": -0.7573988437652588, "top": [[" kernel", -0.7573988437652588], [" driver", -0.7658674716949463]]}, {"t": " version", "lp": -0.6490156054496765, "top": [[" version", -0.6490156054496765], ["/d", -0.8348352313041687]]}, {"t": " and", "lp": -0.6414808630943298, "top": [[" and", -0.6414808630943298], [" or", -0.9544024467468262]]}, {"t": " any", "lp": -1.2521134614944458, "top": [[" any", -1.2521134614944458], [" what", -1.6391621828079224]]}, {"t": " error", "lp": -0.647999107837677, "top": [[" error", -0.647999107837677], [" relevant", -2.152143716812134]]}, {"t": " messages", "lp": -0.08482194691896439, "top": [[" messages", -0.08482194691896439], [" logs", -3.478386163711548]]}, {"t": " you", "lp": -0.46075308322906494, "top": [[" you", -0.46075308322906494], [" or", -1.0836875438690186]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) context switching is handled, which affects how the GPU distributes work across its compute units.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode = 0 (default)**\n- Uses the default hardware context switching behavior\n- The GPU manages CCS allocation automatically\n\n**ccs_mode = 1**\n- Forces a specific CCS allocation strategy\n- Tends to pin certain cores for dedicated use\n\n**ccs_mode = 2**\n- Alternative allocation scheme, often used to work around specific scheduling issues\n\n## When to Use ccs_mode = 4\n\nThis is where I need to be careful about accuracy. The `ccs_mode = 4` option is specifically documented in the context of **CUDA/ROCm-style partitioning** or **isolating CCS cores** for particular workloads.\n\nHowever, I want to flag: I'm not fully certain of the exact semantics of `ccs_mode = 4` for the Arc/xe driver specifically. The CCS mode documentation is somewhat sparse and can vary between driver versions.\n\n## My Recommendation\n\nFor accurate, version-specific details, I'd suggest:\n\n1. **Check the kernel module documentation:**\n   ```bash\n   zcat /usr/share/doc/linux-modules-$(uname -r)/config.gz 2>/dev/null | grep -i ccs\n   ```\n\n2. **Look at the xe driver source or docs:**\n   ```bash\n   modprobe xe ccs_mode=4\n   cat /proc/cmdline\n   ```\n\n3. **Consult the latest Intel Xe driver documentation** on GitHub, as these parameters evolve.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to help you investigate the specific mode values on your system? If you share your kernel version and any error messages you"}